\begin_layout Standard
This is the third match of the game.
 Exp4 sets θ = π/2 · (pairs found / 8),
 so the third pair has θ = 3π/16 = 0.59 rad.
 The ideal values for this pair are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Concurrence:
 sin θ ≈ 0.56.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bloch vector length:
 cos θ ≈ 0.83.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Witness:
 1 + 2 sin θ ≈ 2.11.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I checked the code for the trend chart,
 and it has a problem you should know about.
 On hardware the curve does not plot concurrence,
 because a real device cannot give it.
 It plots witness / 3 as a proxy (c = witness / 3.0),
 while the axis still says “Concurrence”.
 The proxy has a floor of 1/3 even for a product state,
 and the ideal values for the first three pairs are 0.46,
 0.59 and 0.70.
 That is why the curve starts at 0.46 and not near 0.2 as in the simulator.
 The measured points (0.46,
 0.58,
 0.66) follow the ideal proxy closely,
 so the data are fine and only the label is misleading.
 In the thesis text below I describe it as a witness-based indicator.
 I can relabel the axis in the code if you want.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Text for the thesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure X.
 Experiment 4 executed on a real quantum processor,
 third match:
 partially entangled pair with its witness,
 single-qubit Bloch vectors and witness-based trend across matches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure X shows the result window of Experiment 4 on a real IBM quantum processor after the third pair has been found.
 The title box reports the rotation angle θ = 0.59 rad,
 which in this experiment grows deterministically with the number of matches,
 and the corresponding state cos(0.29)|00⟩ + sin(0.29)|11⟩ before the sign encoding.
 It also reports the entanglement witness,
 the noise index,
 and the statement that joint amplitudes are unavailable on real hardware.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measured witness is 1.97.
 The ideal value for this angle is 1 + 2 sin θ ≈ 2.11,
 so the hardware recovers about 93% of the expected correlation strength.
 The value is clearly above the separable bound of 1,
 which confirms that the pair is entangled.
 The noise index is 6.5%,
 corresponding to an average distance of about 0.13 between the measured and ideal Bloch vectors.
 This is the highest value among the experiments presented so far (1.7% in Experiment 1,
 3.8% in Experiment 2,
 3.7% in Experiment 3).
 A possible explanation is a variation in the calibration of the device between the runs,
 but the data alone do not allow this to be established.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two Bloch spheres show vectors of length 0.86 and 0.84,
 against an ideal length of 0.83.
 The vectors are still long,
 since at this stage of the game the entanglement is moderate and each qubit retains a large part of its individual polarization.
 The two arrows point in nearly opposite directions along the vertical axis,
 as in the simulation,
 and are both slightly tilted away from it.
 The tilt is a transverse component caused by noise,
 and it is responsible for most of the noise index,
 since the lengths themselves are close to the ideal ones.
 Compared with Experiment 2,
 where θ = 1.08 rad gave vectors of length 0.5,
 the longer vectors here are the visual counterpart of the smaller θ.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The graph on the right summarizes the three matches so far.
 On hardware the exact concurrence cannot be computed,
 so the plotted quantity is the witness divided by 3,
 which acts as an indicator that grows with entanglement.
 Its values are about 0.46,
 0.58 and 0.66,
 against the ideal values of 0.46,
 0.59 and 0.70 for the same indicator.
 The curve increases monotonically and follows the ideal trend,
 with the largest deviation,
 about 0.04,
 at the third point.
 This quantity differs from concurrence,
 whose ideal values for the same pairs would be 0.20,
 0.38 and 0.56.
 The indicator never falls below 1/3,
 even for a product state,
 so its absolute values should not be compared directly with the simulator curve.
 The comparison must be made on the trend.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 the hardware reproduces the progressive growth of entanglement along the game.
 The witness and the vector lengths agree with the ideal values at the level of a few percent,
 and the deviation is larger than in the previous experiments,
 consistently with its higher noise index.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Experiment 5:
 maximally entangled state exhibiting
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure X.
 Experiment 5 executed on a real quantum processor:
 phase-entangled state with its single-qubit Bloch vectors and two-qubit correlators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Figure X shows the result window of Experiment 5 when the circuit is executed on a real IBM quantum processor.
 The title box reports the state produced for the matched card,
 (|00⟩ − |01⟩ + |10⟩ + |11⟩)/2,
 followed by the witness and the noise index.
 A third line states that the joint amplitudes are unavailable on real hardware.
 In the simulator version,
 the same state is shown through a density matrix grid in which every cell is populated,
 with a characteristic pattern of signs.
 That panel cannot be reconstructed from measurement statistics alone and is therefore replaced on hardware by the single-qubit Bloch spheres.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This experiment differs from the previous ones in an important respect.
 The state is entangled only through its phases.
 The probabilities of the four outcomes are all equal to 0.25,
 so a measurement in the computational basis gives no sign of correlation.
 The entanglement is revealed only by the correlators on the right.
 The ideal values are ⟨XX⟩ = 0,
 ⟨YY⟩ = −1 and ⟨ZZ⟩ = 0,
 and the measured values are 0.04,
 −0.96 and 0.10.
 The dominant correlator is therefore recovered with an error of only 4%,
 and the two correlators that should vanish stay below 0.10.
 The negative sign of ⟨YY⟩ is reproduced correctly,
 which shows that the hardware preserves the phase structure of the state and not only the populations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The witness,
 defined as the sum of the absolute values of the three correlators,
 is 1.10.
 Unlike in Experiments 2 to 4,
 this quantity cannot be used here to certify entanglement.
 For this state the ideal value is 1.0,
 which coincides with the separable bound,
 because only one correlator is different from zero.
 The value 1.10 is therefore compatible with the ideal one,
 and the small excess comes from the residual values of ⟨XX⟩ and ⟨ZZ⟩.
 The evidence for entanglement comes instead from the single-qubit vectors.
 Their length is 0.07 for both qubits,
 against an ideal value of exactly zero.
 For a pure global state,
 completely mixed single-qubit states imply maximal entanglement.
 The near-zero length together with the strong ⟨YY⟩ correlation identifies a state that is highly entangled,
 even though the witness cannot show it.
 The noise index of 3.5% corresponds to this residual length of about 0.07,
 and is similar to the values of 3.7% and 3.8% found for Experiments 3 and 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 the real processor reproduces the phase-entangled state of Experiment 5 with good accuracy.
 The correlators are within 0.10 of their ideal values,
 the vectors are almost at the centre of the spheres,
 and the sign structure is preserved.
 The experiment also shows a limit of the witness,
 which for this class of states stays at the separable bound even though the entanglement is maximal.
\end_layout

